ied\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_run_dir\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181255226823Z-fact-the-alpha-1-source-model-has-exact-\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181255226823Z-fact-the-alpha-1-source-model-has-exact-/codex.log\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_events_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181255226823Z-fact-the-alpha-1-source-model-has-exact-/codex.events.jsonl\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_stderr_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181255226823Z-fact-the-alpha-1-source-model-has-exact-/codex.stderr.log\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_log_manifest\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181255226823Z-fact-the-alpha-1-source-model-has-exact-/log_manifest.json\\\",\\n  \\\"created_at\\\": \\\"2026-06-19T18:16:36.434806Z\\\",\\n  \\\"verifier\\\": \\\"iteris.codex_verification_agent\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T070357651402Z-assembly-stage-3-terminal-report-results/codex.events.jsonl:38:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' verification/results/verify-20260619T181650715321Z-fact-a-positive-augmented-zeroth-order-e.json\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"iteris.verification_result.v0\\\",\\n  \\\"request_id\\\": \\\"verify-20260619T181650715321Z-fact-a-positive-augmented-zeroth-order-e\\\",\\n  \\\"backend\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"fact\\\",\\n  \\\"claim\\\": \\\"A positive augmented zeroth-order energy gives a Gronwall estimate for bounded nonnegative time-independent absorptions in the alpha=1 source model.\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"accepted\\\",\\n  \\\"passed\\\": true,\\n  \\\"strict_verdict\\\": \\\"correct\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Correct. The source model defines the alpha=1 Maxwell-PML system with PEC boundary condition, positive material parameters, and time-independent nonnegative absorptions. The predecessor alpha=1 electromagnetic-plus-polarization balance is already verified and also checks directly against the source equations: PEC cancels the Maxwell boundary flux, and E P_t gives the positive chi^{-1} P^2/2 contribution plus tau/chi dissipation. The cited Gronwall artifact then adds the L2 derivatives of P_{z,1}, P_{z,2}, Q_x, and Q_y, each matching the source auxiliary equations. After dropping the nonpositive E^2, P_t^2, Q_x^2, and Q_y^2 terms, the remaining sign-indefinite H-square terms and mixed products are all bounded by Young's inequality using the energy weights epsilon_0 epsilon_infty, mu, chi^{-1}, and the unweighted auxiliary norms. The required coefficients are controlled by the stated material parameters and L_infty norms of sigma_x, sigma_y, sigma_x sigma_y, sigma_x(sigma_y-sigma_x), and sigma_y(sigma_x-sigma_y). Thus dE_0/dt <= C_G E_0 and Gronwall gives E_0(t) <= exp(C_G t) E_0(0).\\\",\\n  \\\"critical_errors\\\": [],\\n  \\\"gaps\\\": [],\\n  \\\"repair_hints\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"checked_artifacts\\\": [\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-augmented-gronwall-energy.md\\\",\\n    \\\"artifacts/proofs/alpha1_constant_pml_cases.md\\\",\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-alpha1-algebra-balance.md\\\",\\n    \\\"artifacts/proofs/alpha1_algebra_balance.md\\\",\\n    \\\"verification/results/verify-20260619T180514214437Z-fact-alpha-1-pec-balance-and-shifted-mag.json\\\",\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-run-20260619T173930696222Z-source-problem.md\\\",\\n    \\\"sources/energy_functional_problem.tex\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"checked_fact_ids\\\": [\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:augmented-gronwall-energy\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:alpha1-algebra-balance\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:run-20260619T173930696222Z:source-problem\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"primary_fact_ids\\\": [],\\n  \\\"target_artifact\\\": null,\\n  \\\"executor\\\": \\\"codex\\\",\\n  \\\"verification_scope\\\": \\\"codex_agent\\\",\\n  \\\"claim_ceiling_after_verification\\\": \\\"verified\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_run_dir\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181650715321Z-fact-a-positive-augmented-zeroth-order-e\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181650715321Z-fact-a-positive-augmented-zeroth-order-e/codex.log\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_events_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181650715321Z-fact-a-positive-augmented-zeroth-order-e/codex.events.jsonl\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_stderr_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181650715321Z-fact-a-positive-augmented-zeroth-order-e/codex.stderr.log\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_log_manifest\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260619T181650715321Z-fact-a-positive-augmented-zeroth-order-e/log_manifest.json\\\",\\n  \\\"created_at\\\": \\\"2026-06-19T18:20:42.329731Z\\\",\\n  \\\"verifier\\\": \\\"iteris.codex_verification_agent\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T070357651402Z-assembly-stage-3-terminal-report-results/codex.events.jsonl:40:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' verification/results/verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw.json\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"iteris.verification_result.v0\\\",\\n  \\\"request_id\\\": \\\"verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw\\\",\\n  \\\"backend\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"fact\\\",\\n  \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The covariantly damped alpha=1 Maxwell-Debye absorbing-layer model has a positive monotone energy identity.\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"accepted\\\",\\n  \\\"passed\\\": true,\\n  \\\"strict_verdict\\\": \\\"correct\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Correct within the stated claim scope. The cited fact and proof define a modified covariantly damped alpha=1 Maxwell-Debye absorbing-layer model with variables E, H_x, H_y, P, nonnegative time-independent rho_x,rho_y, rho=rho_x+rho_y, and D_rho=partial_t+rho. Multiplying the field equations by E,H_x,H_y gives the energy derivative plus the nonnegative electric and magnetic damping terms; the Maxwell principal part integrates to the boundary flux int_dOmega E(H_y n_x - H_x n_y) ds, which vanishes under PEC E=0. The covariant Debye law P+tau D_rho P=chi E gives E D_rho P=(2 chi)^{-1} partial_t(P^2)+rho chi^{-1}P^2+tau chi^{-1}|D_rho P|^2, so the proposed positive energy has exactly the displayed monotone identity. The predecessor facts about the original source model are verified and are used only as context/motivation for the repair; the artifact clearly states that the covariantly damped model is a modified absorbing model, not an algebraically identical proof for the old eight-variable unsplit PML and not a proof of exact perfect matching.\\\",\\n  \\\"critical_errors\\\": [],\\n  \\\"gaps\\\": [],\\n  \\\"repair_hints\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"checked_artifacts\\\": [\\n    \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw/request.json\\\",\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-covariantly-damped-debye-energy.md\\\",\\n    \\\"artifacts/proofs/stage2_repair_design.md\\\",\\n    \\\"sources/energy_functional_problem.tex\\\",\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-alpha1-algebra-balance.md\\\",\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-edge-pml-linear-obstruction.md\\\",\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-augmented-gronwall-energy.md\\\",\\n    \\\"verification/results/verify-20260619T180514214437Z-fact-alpha-1-pec-balance-and-shifted-mag.json\\\",\\n    \\\"verification/results/verify-20260619T181255226823Z-fact-the-alpha-1-source-model-has-exact-.json\\\",\\n    \\\"verification/results/verify-20260619T181650715321Z-fact-a-positive-augmented-zeroth-order-e.json\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"checked_fact_ids\\\": [\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:covariantly-damped-debye-energy\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:alpha1-algebra-balance\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:edge-pml-linear-obstruction\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:augmented-gronwall-energy\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:run-20260619T173930696222Z:source-problem\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"primary_fact_ids\\\": [],\\n  \\\"target_artifact\\\": null,\\n  \\\"executor\\\": \\\"codex\\\",\\n  \\\"verification_scope\\\": \\\"codex_agent\\\",\\n  \\\"claim_ceiling_after_verification\\\": \\\"verified\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_run_dir\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw/codex.log\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_events_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw/codex.events.jsonl\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_stderr_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw/codex.stderr.log\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_log_manifest\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw/log_manifest.json\\\",\\n  \\\"created_at\\\": \\\"2026-06-20T02:27:38.310502Z\\\",\\n  \\\"verifier\\\": \\\"iteris.codex_verification_agent\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T070357651402Z-assembly-stage-3-terminal-report-results/codex.events.jsonl:43:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' sources/energy_functional_problem.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\\\documentclass[UTF8,a4paper,12pt]{ctexart}\\n\\\\input{macros}\\n\\n\\\\title{Problem Sheet for Iteris: Energy Functionals for 2D Maxwell--PML}\\n\\\\author{Prepared source problem}\\n\\\\date{\\\\today}\\n\\n\\\\begin{document}\\n\\\\maketitle\\n\\n\\\\begin{abstract}\\nThis note is a self-contained source problem for an Iteris run.  The system to be analyzed is the two-dimensional TM Maxwell--PML model with polarization \\\\(P\\\\), PML auxiliary variables \\\\(P_{z,1},P_{z,2},Q_x,Q_y\\\\), and perfectly conducting boundary condition.  The first target is to find a genuinely monotone decreasing energy functional in two space dimensions.  If such a monotone energy cannot be found, the fallback target is to find a positive energy functional that proves boundedness/no abnormal long-time growth, in the same spirit as the long-time-stability role of MPML.  The non-fractional case should be understood as the special case \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\) of the displayed polarization law.\\n\\\\end{abstract}\\n\\n\\\\section{Primary Goal}\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\Om\\\\subset\\\\R^2\\\\) be a rectangular computational domain containing a physical region and a PML layer.  The absorption parameters are\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\sx=\\\\sx(x)\\\\ge0,\\\\qquad \\\\sy=\\\\sy(y)\\\\ge0,\\n\\\\]\\nor, in a harder variant,\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\sx=\\\\sx(x,y)\\\\ge0,\\\\qquad \\\\sy=\\\\sy(x,y)\\\\ge0.\\n\\\\]\\nThe main goal is to analyze the two-dimensional system in Section~\\\\ref{sec:model} and construct an energy functional \\\\(\\\\Ecal(t)\\\\).\\n\\n\\\\begin{enumerate}\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Strong target: monotone decreasing energy.}\\nFind a positive energy functional such that\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\frac{\\\\dd}{\\\\dd t}\\\\Ecal(t)=-\\\\Dcal(t)\\\\le0.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the preferred outcome.\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Fallback target: bounded/non-growing energy.}\\nIf no monotone energy can be found, find a positive energy functional that rules out abnormal growth from the PML layer, for example\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\Ecal(t)\\\\le C\\\\,\\\\Ecal(0)\\n\\\\]\\nwith \\\\(C\\\\) independent of long time if possible.  If only a Gronwall estimate\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\frac{\\\\dd}{\\\\dd t}\\\\Ecal(t)\\\\le C_\\\\sigma\\\\Ecal(t)\\n\\\\]\\nis available, identify the precise terms responsible for the possible growth and whether they are coefficient-gradient terms, corner-PML terms, or algebraic sign-indefinite terms.\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Useful obstruction.}\\nIf the strong target fails for natural quadratic energies, prove or at least sharply identify the obstruction.  An obstruction result is valuable if it explains which part of the PML coupling prevents monotonicity.\\n\\\\end{enumerate}\\n\\nThe energy may include weighted \\\\(L^2\\\\) norms, cross terms, shifted variables, time-integrated auxiliary variables, polarization energy, fractional-memory energy, and derivative terms if they are mathematically justified.\\n\\n\\\\section{Model to Analyze}\\n\\\\label{sec:model}\\n\\nThe model is the system shown in the supplied manuscript image.  Write\\n\\\\[\\n  E=E_z,\\\\qquad \\\\epsilon_\\\\infty=\\\\epsilon_{r,\\\\infty},\\\\qquad\\n  \\\\epsilon_s=\\\\epsilon_{r,s},\\\\qquad \\\\mu=\\\\mu_0\\\\mu_r,\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\chi=\\\\epsilon_0(\\\\epsilon_s-\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\nThe unknowns are\\n\\\\[\\n  E,\\\\quad H_x,\\\\quad H_y,\\\\quad P,\\\\quad P_{z,1},\\\\quad P_{z,2},\\\\quad Q_x,\\\\quad Q_y.\\n\\\\]\\nThe PML system is\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty\\\\,\\\\partial_t E\\n&= \\\\partial_xH_y-\\\\partial_yH_x\\n   -\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty(\\\\sx+\\\\sy)E\\n   -\\\\partial_tP-\\\\frac{\\\\sx+\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}P\\n   +P_{z,1}+P_{z,2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\mu\\\\,\\\\partial_tH_x\\n&= -\\\\partial_yE-\\\\mu\\\\frac{\\\\sy-\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0}H_x+Q_x,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\mu\\\\,\\\\partial_tH_y\\n&= \\\\partial_xE-\\\\mu\\\\frac{\\\\sx-\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}H_y+Q_y,\\\\\\\\\\nP+\\\\tau^\\\\alpha\\\\partial_t^\\\\alpha P\\n&=\\\\chi E,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tP_{z,1}\\n&=-\\\\frac{\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty}{\\\\epsilon_0}\\\\sx\\\\sy E,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tP_{z,2}\\n&=-\\\\frac{\\\\sx\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0^2}P,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tQ_x\\n&=-\\\\frac{\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0}Q_x\\n  +\\\\frac{\\\\mu\\\\sx(\\\\sy-\\\\sx)}{\\\\epsilon_0^2}H_x,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tQ_y\\n&=-\\\\frac{\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}Q_y\\n  +\\\\frac{\\\\mu\\\\sy(\\\\sx-\\\\sy)}{\\\\epsilon_0^2}H_y.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\label{eq:main-model}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nHere \\\\(\\\\partial_t^\\\\alpha\\\\) should be interpreted as a Caputo-type fractional derivative when \\\\(0<\\\\alpha<1\\\\).  The non-fractional case requested by the user is the case\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\alpha=1,\\\\qquad\\n  P+\\\\tau\\\\partial_tP=\\\\chi E.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the first stage of the analysis should use \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\), not a model with \\\\(P\\\\) removed.\\n\\nThe boundary condition is the perfectly conducting boundary condition\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\widehat n\\\\times E=0\\\\qquad\\\\text{on }\\\\partial\\\\Om.\\n\\\\]\\nFor the two-dimensional TM model this should be interpreted as\\n\\\\[\\n  E=E_z=0\\\\qquad\\\\text{on }\\\\partial\\\\Om,\\n\\\\]\\nso that the Maxwell principal part has no positive boundary contribution.  If a proof uses a different boundary condition, it must state that choice explicitly.\\n\\n\\\\begin{remark}\\nThe parameters \\\\(\\\\epsilon_0,\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty,\\\\epsilon_s,\\\\mu,\\\\tau\\\\) are positive, and \\\\(\\\\chi>0\\\\) when \\\\(\\\\epsilon_s>\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty\\\\).  The absorption parameters are time-independent and nonnegative.  The first serious cases to analyze are constant \\\\(\\\\sx,\\\\sy\\\\), then separated variable coefficients \\\\(\\\\sx(x),\\\\sy(y)\\\\), and only then general \\\\(\\\\sx(x,y),\\\\sy(x,y)\\\\).\\n\\\\end{remark}\\n\\n\\\\section{First Energy Balance to Verify}\\n\\nStart with the electromagnetic part\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\Ecal_{\\\\rm em}(t)=\\\\frac12\\\\int_\\\\Om\\n  \\\\left(\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty E^2+\\\\mu H_x^2+\\\\mu H_y^2\\\\right)\\\\dd x\\\\dd y.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Check the electromagnetic energy derivative]\\nUsing \\\\eqref{eq:main-model} and the PEC boundary condition, verify that the Maxwell principal part cancels by integration by parts, and that\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\frac{\\\\dd}{\\\\dd t}\\\\Ecal_{\\\\rm em}\\n=\\\\int_\\\\Om\\\\Big[\\n&-\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty(\\\\sx+\\\\sy)E^2\\n-E\\\\partial_tP\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\sx+\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}EP\\n+E(P_{z,1}+P_{z,2})\\\\\\\\\\n&-\\\\frac{\\\\mu}{\\\\epsilon_0}(\\\\sy-\\\\sx)H_x^2\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\mu}{\\\\epsilon_0}(\\\\sx-\\\\sy)H_y^2\\n+H_xQ_x+H_yQ_y\\n\\\\Big]\\\\dd x\\\\dd y .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\label{eq:em-balance}\\n\\\\]\\nThe terms involving \\\\(H_x,H_y\\\\) are sign-indefinite when \\\\(\\\\sx\\\\ne\\\\sy\\\\).  The terms involving \\\\(P,P_{z,1},P_{z,2}\\\\) are the electric/PML auxiliary coupling terms that must be absorbed by a refined energy.\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\section{The \\\\texorpdfstring{\\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\)}{alpha=1} Polarization Identity}\\n\\nFor the non-fractional case \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n  P+\\\\tau\\\\partial_tP=\\\\chi E,\\n\\\\]\\nso\\n\\\\[\\n  E=\\\\frac{P+\\\\tau\\\\partial_tP}{\\\\chi}.\\n\\\\]\\nThis gives the useful identity\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  E\\\\,\\\\partial_tP\\n  =\\\\frac{1}{2\\\\chi}\\\\frac{\\\\dd}{\\\\dd t}\\\\|P\\\\|_{L^2(\\\\Om)}^2\\n   +\\\\frac{\\\\tau}{\\\\chi}\\\\|\\\\partial_tP\\\\|_{L^2(\\\\Om)}^2.\\n\\\\label{eq:alpha-one-dissipation}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Use the alpha=1 identity]\\nUse \\\\eqref{eq:alpha-one-dissipation} to decide whether the energy\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\Ecal_{\\\\rm em+P}\\n  =\\\\Ecal_{\\\\rm em}+\\\\frac{1}{2\\\\chi}\\\\|P\\\\|_{L^2(\\\\Om)}^2\\n\\\\]\\nis the right starting point.  Then identify the remaining troublesome electric auxiliary terms:\\n\\\\[\\n  -\\\\frac{\\\\sx+\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}EP,\\\\qquad\\n  E P_{z,1},\\\\qquad\\n  E P_{z,2},\\\\qquad\\n  \\\\partial_tP_{z,1},\\\\qquad\\n  \\\\partial_tP_{z,2}.\\n\\\\]\\nTry to absorb them using cross terms, shifted variables, or time-integrated variables.\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\section{Magnetic Auxiliary Variable Shift}\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\n  a_x=\\\\frac{\\\\sy-\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0},\\\\qquad\\n  a_y=\\\\frac{\\\\sx-\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0},\\\\qquad\\n  b_x=\\\\frac{\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0},\\\\qquad\\n  b_y=\\\\frac{\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe magnetic equations and \\\\(Q_x,Q_y\\\\) equations suggest the shifted variables\\n\\\\[\\n  M_x=Q_x-\\\\mu a_xH_x,\\\\qquad\\n  M_y=Q_y-\\\\mu a_yH_y.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Verify shifted magnetic identities]\\nStarting from \\\\eqref{eq:main-model}, verify\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\partial_tM_x\\n&= a_x\\\\,\\\\partial_yE-\\\\frac{\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}M_x,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tM_y\\n&= -a_y\\\\,\\\\partial_xE-\\\\frac{\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0}M_y.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\label{eq:magnetic-shift}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nThen test whether an energy written in terms of \\\\(H_x,H_y,M_x,M_y\\\\), rather than \\\\(H_x,H_y,Q_x,Q_y\\\\), has a cleaner dissipation structure.\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\begin{remark}\\nIf \\\\(\\\\sx,\\\\sy\\\\) are spatially variable, \\\\eqref{eq:magnetic-shift} itself does not produce \\\\(\\\\partial_t\\\\sigma\\\\) terms, because the coefficients are time-independent.  However, integrated energy identities may produce \\\\(\\\\partial_x\\\\sx\\\\), \\\\(\\\\partial_y\\\\sy\\\\), or general \\\\(\\\\nabla\\\\sigma_i\\\\) terms after integration by parts.  These terms are likely to be one source of bounded-but-not-monotone estimates.\\n\\\\end{remark}\\n\\n\\\\section{Electric PML Auxiliary Variables}\\n\\nThe variables \\\\(P_{z,1}\\\\) and \\\\(P_{z,2}\\\\) satisfy\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\partial_tP_{z,1}=-\\\\frac{\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty}{\\\\epsilon_0}\\\\sx\\\\sy E,\\\\qquad\\n  \\\\partial_tP_{z,2}=-\\\\frac{\\\\sx\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0^2}P.\\n\\\\]\\nThey appear in the \\\\(E\\\\)-equation as \\\\(E(P_{z,1}+P_{z,2})\\\\).  This resembles the need for time-integrated or cross-term energies in PML analyses.\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Handle Pz1 and Pz2]\\nTry the following routes:\\n\\\\begin{enumerate}\\n\\\\item define time-integrated variables\\n\\\\[\\n  R_1(t)=\\\\int_0^tP_{z,1}(s)\\\\dd s,\\\\qquad\\n  R_2(t)=\\\\int_0^tP_{z,2}(s)\\\\dd s;\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\item add cross terms such as \\\\((E,P_{z,1})\\\\), \\\\((P,P_{z,2})\\\\), or weighted combinations;\\n\\\\item freeze \\\\(\\\\sx,\\\\sy\\\\) as constants and solve the pointwise Lyapunov matrix problem for the electric subsystem \\\\((E,P,P_{z,1},P_{z,2})\\\\);\\n\\\\item identify whether the corner factor \\\\(\\\\sx\\\\sy\\\\) is the essential obstruction.\\n\\\\end{enumerate}\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\section{Baffet-Type Energy Design Clue}\\n\\nBaffet--Grote--Imperiale--Kachanovska proved monotone energy decay for a second-order wave-equation PML with constant damping coefficients\\\\cite{baffetGroteImperialeKachanovska2019}.  Their main lesson is methodological: do not start from the naive energy alone.  In two dimensions they introduce\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\lambda=\\\\nabla u+\\\\phi,\\\\qquad\\n  a=\\\\operatorname{tr}\\\\Gamma_1,\\\\qquad\\n  b=\\\\det\\\\Gamma_1,\\n\\\\]\\nand use an energy of the form\\n\\\\[\\n  E[u,\\\\phi]=\\\\frac12\\\\left(\\n  \\\\|\\\\partial_tu+a u\\\\|^2+\\\\|\\\\nabla u+\\\\phi\\\\|^2\\n  +\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|^2_{a^{-1}\\\\Gamma_1}+\\\\|u\\\\|^2_b\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\nIn three dimensions they further introduce time-integrated auxiliary variables \\\\(\\\\Phi,\\\\Psi\\\\) and a shifted variable \\\\(q\\\\).\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Translate the Baffet idea to \\\\eqref{eq:main-model}]\\nLook for Maxwell--PML analogue