E-P}{\\\\tau}\\n\\\\]\\nto rewrite \\\\eqref{eq:main-model} as a first-order system for\\n\\\\[\\n  U=(E,H_x,H_y,P,P_{z,1},P_{z,2},Q_x,Q_y)^T.\\n\\\\]\\nAfter Fourier transform in space,\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\partial_t\\\\widehat U=A(\\\\sx,\\\\sy,k_x,k_y)\\\\widehat U.\\n\\\\]\\nSearch for a Hermitian positive matrix \\\\(G\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\n  G A + A^*G\\\\le0.\\n\\\\]\\nIf such a \\\\(G\\\\) is independent of \\\\(k\\\\), it suggests a local \\\\(L^2\\\\) energy.  If \\\\(G\\\\) depends polynomially on \\\\(k\\\\), it suggests derivative energies.  If no positive \\\\(G\\\\) exists in a natural class, record the obstruction.\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Algebraic feasibility test]\\nPerform the pointwise Lyapunov search in the following order:\\n\\\\begin{enumerate}\\n\\\\item no PML: \\\\(\\\\sx=\\\\sy=0\\\\);\\n\\\\item edge PML: \\\\(\\\\sx>0,\\\\sy=0\\\\);\\n\\\\item edge PML: \\\\(\\\\sx=0,\\\\sy>0\\\\);\\n\\\\item symmetric corner: \\\\(\\\\sx=\\\\sy>0\\\\);\\n\\\\item asymmetric corner: \\\\(\\\\sx>0,\\\\sy>0,\\\\sx\\\\ne\\\\sy\\\\).\\n\\\\end{enumerate}\\nFor each case, record whether there is an explicit positive quadratic energy, whether it is monotone, and whether the shifted variables \\\\(M_x,M_y\\\\) or integrated variables improve the result.\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\section{Fractional Case \\\\texorpdfstring{\\\\(0<\\\\alpha<1\\\\)}{0<alpha<1}}\\n\\nThe first stage should analyze \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\).  Only after the \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\) mechanism is understood should the fractional case be considered.  For \\\\(0<\\\\alpha<1\\\\), the equation\\n\\\\[\\n  P+\\\\tau^\\\\alpha\\\\partial_t^\\\\alpha P=\\\\chi E\\n\\\\]\\nrequires fractional-memory tools.  A possible route is to use a diffusive representation or complete monotonicity to produce a positive memory energy.\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Fractional memory extension]\\nAssuming the \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\) energy has been found or its obstruction understood, determine whether the fractional polarization can be added as a positive memory energy.  Do not assume the correct memory weight; derive it from the Cole--Cole law.  If the fractional part is not needed for the current goal, explicitly postpone it and keep the main theorem for \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\).\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\section{Deliverables Requested from Iteris}\\n\\nAn Iteris run should aim to produce one of the following terminal artifacts.\\n\\n\\\\begin{enumerate}\\n\\\\item A proved monotone energy identity for \\\\eqref{eq:main-model} with \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\), under clearly stated assumptions.\\n\\\\item A proved bounded-energy or no-abnormal-growth estimate for \\\\eqref{eq:main-model} with \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\), if monotonicity is unavailable.\\n\\\\item A verified obstruction showing why a natural class of quadratic energies cannot be monotone for the full two-dimensional corner PML.\\n\\\\item A staged result: edge PML solved, corner PML reduced to a finite-dimensional Lyapunov condition, fractional \\\\(0<\\\\alpha<1\\\\) case postponed with precise reason.\\n\\\\end{enumerate}\\n\\nEvery result should state:\\n\\\\begin{itemize}\\n\\\\item exact model equations used;\\n\\\\item whether \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\) or \\\\(0<\\\\alpha<1\\\\);\\n\\\\item boundary assumptions, especially how PEC is used;\\n\\\\item assumptions on \\\\(\\\\sx,\\\\sy\\\\);\\n\\\\item proposed energy functional;\\n\\\\item proof of positivity or explanation of why positivity fails;\\n\\\\item exact energy identity or inequality;\\n\\\\item whether the result is monotone, bounded without abnormal growth, Gronwall-type, or an obstruction result.\\n\\\\end{itemize}\\n\\n\\\\bibliographystyle{plainurl}\\n\\\\bibliography{../references/references}\\n\\n\\\\end{document}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T070357651402Z-assembly-stage-3-terminal-report-results/codex.events.jsonl:52:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_26\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' prompts/run_energy_design_v3_perfect_matching.sh\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\\nset -euo pipefail\\n\\nPROJECT_DIR=\\\"$(cd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")/..\\\" && pwd)\\\"\\ncd \\\"$PROJECT_DIR\\\"\\n\\nif [ ! -d results ]; then\\n  echo \\\"Error: expected existing results/ directory in $PROJECT_DIR\\\" >&2\\n  exit 1\\nfi\\n\\nGOAL=$(cat <<'EOF'\\nStage-3 goal for the 2D Maxwell--PML energy-functional project.\\n\\nContext:\\nThe previous report results/energy_design_v2_report.md proposed a modified\\ncovariantly damped Maxwell--Debye absorbing-layer model and verified\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal E_\\\\rho(t)\\\\le \\\\mathcal E_\\\\rho(0),\\\\qquad t\\\\ge0.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is useful, but it is not enough for the next research step.\\n\\nThe next goal is not merely to re-state that the energy is bounded or\\nnon-increasing.  The next goal is:\\n\\n1. Verify whether the proposed modified PML model is actually perfectly\\n   matched.\\n2. If it is perfectly matched, give a complete formula-level proof.\\n3. If it is not perfectly matched, identify the exact mismatch and propose a\\n   repaired PML model that is closer to a standard stretched-coordinate,\\n   Berenger, CFS-PML, MPML, or Baffet-type construction.\\n4. For the accepted or repaired model, construct a genuinely monotone\\n   decreasing/dissipative energy functional and show the full derivation of its\\n   energy identity.\\n5. If no such monotone energy can exist for the model under consideration,\\n   provide a formula-level obstruction, not just a qualitative statement.\\n\\nImportant distinction:\\n- \\\"Non-increasing\\\" means dE/dt <= 0.\\n- \\\"Strictly decreasing\\\" or \\\"genuinely dissipative\\\" should mean\\n  dE/dt = -D(t) with D(t) >= 0 and with a clear explanation of when D(t)=0.\\n- If strict decay of the total energy on the whole computational domain is\\n  impossible because waves may remain in the undamped physical region, state\\n  this precisely and prove the obstruction with a concrete class of solutions.\\n- In that case, the acceptable target is a sharp dissipative identity plus a\\n  no-abnormal-growth/long-time-boundedness result, and the report must explain\\n  why this is the strongest possible statement.\\n\\nRequired sources to read before drawing conclusions:\\n1. sources/energy_functional_problem.tex\\n2. results/energy_functional_report_verified.md\\n3. results/energy_design_v2_report.md\\n4. results/energy_design_v2_report_verified.md\\n5. references/references.bib\\n6. The local PDFs in references/, especially:\\n   - references/Joly - 2012 - An elementary introduction to the construction and the analysis of perfectly matched layers for time.pdf\\n   - references/Baffet ... Energy Decay and Stability of a Perfectly Matched Layer For the Wave Equation.pdf\\n   - references/Be*cache ... Convergence analysis of time-domain PMLS for 2D electromagnetic wave propagation in dispersive waveg.pdf\\n   - references/Be*cache ... On a surprising instability result of Perfectly Matched Layers for Maxwell's equations in 3D media w.pdf\\n   - references/Huang ... explicit unconditionally stable finite element scheme for an equivalent Berenger's PML model.pdf\\n   - references/A_Nonconvolutional_Split-Field_Perfectly_Matched_L.pdf\\n   - references/Correia*Jin ... regular PML, CFS-PML, and second-order PML.pdf\\n   - references/Feng ... higher-order perfectly matched layer based on ADE method.pdf\\n   - references/Ping ... multiaxial perfectly matched layer M-PML.pdf\\n   - references/main.pdf\\n   - references/Super.pdf\\n   - references/Thesis.pdf\\n\\nDo not claim that a reference supports a conclusion unless you actually inspect\\nthe relevant model, theorem, proof idea, or formula.  The final report must\\ninclude a \\\"Literature reading record\\\" table listing each cited reference, the\\nsections/pages/formulas inspected, and exactly how it was used.\\n\\nMain technical tasks:\\n\\nA. Define a precise perfect-matching test.\\nStart with a planar interface and constant PML coefficients:\\n- physical half-space: rho_x=rho_y=0 or sigma_x=sigma_y=0;\\n- PML half-space, for example x>0 with rho_x>0, rho_y=0;\\n- tangential Fourier mode exp(i k_y y);\\n- Laplace or time-harmonic ansatz in time.\\n\\nDerive the transformed algebraic system, dispersion relation, normal wave\\nnumber, and wave impedance/admittance on both sides of the interface.\\n\\nPerfect matching should mean that for every outgoing physical mode entering\\nthe layer, the interface conditions admit a transmitted PML mode with zero\\nreflected amplitude.  Equivalently, the PML model should be representable as a\\ncomplex/stretched-coordinate continuation of the physical Maxwell--Debye\\nsystem without changing the interface impedance.\\n\\nB. Test the covariantly damped Maxwell--Debye model from v2.\\nUse the model\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty D_\\\\rho E\\n=\\\\partial_xH_y-\\\\partial_yH_x-D_\\\\rho P,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mu(\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho_y)H_x=-\\\\partial_yE,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mu(\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho_x)H_y=\\\\partial_xE,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nP+\\\\tau D_\\\\rho P=\\\\chi E,\\\\qquad D_\\\\rho=\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho,\\\\quad\\n\\\\rho=\\\\rho_x+\\\\rho_y.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nCheck whether this model is exactly matched in the sense of Task A.\\nIn particular, determine whether replacing the Debye law\\n\\\\[\\nP+\\\\tau\\\\partial_t P=\\\\chi E\\n\\\\]\\nby\\n\\\\[\\nP+\\\\tau(\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho)P=\\\\chi E\\n\\\\]\\npreserves the correct transformed material law or changes the interface\\nimpedance.  If it changes the impedance, compute the mismatch explicitly.\\n\\nC. If the v2 covariantly damped model is not perfectly matched, construct or\\npropose a repaired model.\\nAllowed approaches include:\\n1. Standard stretched-coordinate PML for Maxwell--Debye:\\n   derive it in the Laplace domain first, then introduce auxiliary variables\\n   for the time-domain model.\\n2. Berenger split-field PML or an equivalent unsplit auxiliary-variable form.\\n3. CFS-PML/ADE-PML if frequency shifting is needed for long-time behavior.\\n4. MPML-type damping if standard PML is unstable or not dissipative.\\n5. Baffet-type time-integrated variables or augmented variables to obtain a\\n   positive energy.\\n\\nThe repaired model must state:\\n- exact PDE system;\\n- boundary/interface conditions;\\n- assumptions on sigma_x, sigma_y or rho_x, rho_y;\\n- whether coefficients are constant, piecewise constant, or spatially varying;\\n- whether the model is perfect matching, stable only, or merely absorbing.\\n\\nD. Construct a monotone/dissipative energy functional.\\nFor each serious candidate model, give:\\n1. the exact candidate energy functional;\\n2. positivity proof, including conditions on coefficients and cross terms;\\n3. step-by-step multiplication/integration-by-parts calculation;\\n4. all boundary terms and why they vanish or have a sign;\\n5. final identity\\n   \\\\[\\n   \\\\frac{d}{dt}\\\\mathcal E(t)=-\\\\mathcal D(t)\\n   \\\\]\\n   or final inequality with every remainder term displayed;\\n6. classification:\\n   - strictly decreasing;\\n   - non-increasing with characterized nullspace;\\n   - dissipative but only after adding time-integrated variables;\\n   - uniformly bounded/no abnormal growth;\\n   - Gronwall only;\\n   - impossible, with formula-level obstruction.\\n\\nE. Address the relation to the old eight-variable model.\\nUse the verified old-model obstruction:\\n\\\\[\\nM_x=Q_x+\\\\mu\\\\frac{\\\\sigma_x}{\\\\epsilon_0}H_x=\\\\text{constant}\\n\\\\]\\non the x-edge zero-transverse mode and the resulting linear growth.  Explain\\nwhether the repaired model removes this mode, restricts it by compatibility\\nconditions, or changes the PML equations.\\n\\nF. Discuss Cole--Cole/fractional extension only after the alpha=1 perfect\\nmatching and energy questions are settled.\\nFor the fractional case, do not claim a theorem unless a positive memory\\nrepresentation is explicitly written.  State whether the fractional law helps\\nor cannot help with any electromagnetic/PML obstruction independent of P.\\n\\nRequired final artifact:\\nWrite the final report to:\\nresults/energy_design_v3_perfect_matching_report.md\\n\\nThe report should be written in Chinese, with formulas in LaTeX.\\n\\nRequired report structure:\\n\\n1. Executive summary\\n   - Is the v2 covariantly damped model perfectly matched?\\n   - Is a monotone/dissipative energy obtained?\\n   - What is the strongest verified conclusion?\\n\\n2. Literature reading record\\n   - Table: reference, inspected pages/sections/formulas, relevant result,\\n     how it affects the present problem.\\n\\n3. Perfect matching criterion\\n   - Planar interface setup;\\n   - Laplace/Fourier ansatz;\\n   - dispersion relation;\\n   - impedance/reflection coefficient criterion.\\n\\n4. Perfect matching test for the v2 model\\n   - full algebra;\\n   - conclusion;\\n   - if not matched, exact mismatch.\\n\\n5. Candidate repaired PML models\\n   - at least two candidates if the v2 model fails;\\n   - exact equations and matching/stability status.\\n\\n6. Energy functional construction\\n   - candidate energy;\\n   - positivity;\\n   - detailed derivative calculation;\\n   - final identity or inequality.\\n\\n7. Obstructions and impossibility statements\\n   - include explicit modes or algebraic blocks if monotone strict decay is\\n     impossible.\\n\\n8. Recommendation for the next manuscript step\\n   - which model to pursue;\\n   - what theorem statement looks feasible;\\n   - what remains open.\\n\\nVerification requirements:\\n- Use facts already verified by Iteris where applicable, but do not let them\\n  replace the new perfect-matching derivation.\\n- Verify any new algebraic claim needed for the final conclusion.\\n- If a reference PDF cannot be parsed or read, say so explicitly and mark the\\n  corresponding claim as unverified.\\n- Do not fabricate references, DOI numbers, theorem numbers, or page numbers.\\nEOF\\n)\\n\\niteris run . \\\\\\n  --goal \\\"$GOAL\\\" \\\\\\n  --target-artifact results/energy_design_v3_perfect_matching_report.md \\\\\\n  --foreground \\\\\\n  --new-session\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T070357651402Z-assembly-stage-3-terminal-report-results/codex.events.jsonl:55:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_27\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' verification/results/verify-20260620T023507094543Z-goal_success-read-.iteris-goal_prompt.tx.json\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"iteris.verification_result.v0\\\",\\n  \\\"request_id\\\": \\\"verify-20260620T023507094543Z-goal_success-read-.iteris-goal_prompt.tx\\\",\\n  \\\"backend\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"goal_success\\\",\\n  \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Read .iteris/goal_prompt.txt and execute the second-stage Iteris goal: analyze the verified obstruction in results/energy_functional_report_verified.md without treating it as final success; identify the old-model edge-PML linear-growth term; propose at least three repairs including compatibility restriction, shifted-variable reformulation, and modified damping/PML model; derive candidate energies, positivity, and energy derivative classifications; select the most promising repaired model; write results/energy_design_v2_report.md with obstruction, modified models, exact best-candidate equations, energy functional, positivity proof, energy identity or inequality, classification, and open problems; pass assembly and goal-success verification.\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"accepted\\\",\\n  \\\"passed\\\": true,\\n  \\\"strict_verdict\\\": \\\"correct\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Correct. The source goal is a stage-2 constructive task, not a universal proof that the original eight-variable source PML is monotone: it requires analyzing the verified old edge-PML obstruction, proposing repaired or modified formulations, deriving energies/positivity/derivative classifications, selecting a promising repaired model, and writing results/energy_design_v2_report.md. The terminal report covers that scope. It identifies the undamped shifted magnetic edge mode as the old-model obstruction, gives three repair routes (compatibility restriction, shifted-variable MPML damping, and a covariantly damped Maxwell-Debye absorbing layer), classifies the first two as partial, and selects the covariantly damped model. The selected model's positivity and monotone energy identity are supported by the cited verified fact and by the displayed calculation: PEC cancels the Maxwell flux, and the covariant Debye identity converts E D_rho P into the time derivative of chi^{-1}P^2/2 plus nonnegative rho P^2/chi and tau |D_rho P|^2/chi dissipation. The report does not overclaim exact perfect matching or a monotone energy for the old eight-variable model.\\\",\\n  \\\"critical_errors\\\": [],\\n  \\\"gaps\\\": [],\\n  \\\"repair_hints\\\": \\\"No repair needed.\\\",\\n  \\\"checked_artifacts\\\": [\\n    \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T023507094543Z-goal_success-read-.iteris-goal_prompt.tx/request.json\\\",\\n    \\\".iteris/goal_prompt.txt\\\",\\n    \\\"sources/energy_functional_problem.tex\\\",\\n    \\\"results/energy_functional_report_verified.md\\\",\\n    \\\"results/energy_design_v2_report.md\\\",\\n    \\\"artifacts/proofs/stage2_repair_design.md\\\",\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-covariantly-damped-debye-energy.md\\\",\\n    \\\"memory/facts/fact-2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration-edge-pml-linear-obstruction.md\\\",\\n    \\\"verification/results/verify-20260620T022411192181Z-fact-the-covariantly-damped-alpha-1-maxw.json\\\",\\n    \\\"verification/results/verify-20260619T181255226823Z-fact-the-alpha-1-source-model-has-exact-.json\\\",\\n    \\\"verification/results/verify-20260620T023007094217Z-assembly-assemble-verified-facts-to-answ.json\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"checked_fact_ids\\\": [\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:alpha1-algebra-balance\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:edge-pml-linear-obstruction\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:augmented-gronwall-energy\\\",\\n    \\\"fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:covariantly-damped-debye-energy\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"primary_fact_ids\\\": [],\\n  \\\"target_artifact\\\": \\\"results/energy_design_v2_report.md\\\",\\n  \\\"executor\\\": \\\"codex\\\",\\n  \\\"verification_scope\\\": \\\"codex_agent\\\",\\n  \\\"claim_ceiling_after_verification\\\": \\\"verified\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_run_dir\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T023507094543Z-goal_success-read-.iteris-goal_prompt.tx\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T023507094543Z-goal_success-read-.iteris-goal_prompt.tx/codex.log\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_events_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T023507094543Z-goal_success-read-.iteris-goal_prompt.tx/codex.events.jsonl\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_stderr_log\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T023507094543Z-goal_success-read-.iteris-goal_prompt.tx/codex.stderr.log\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_log_manifest\\\": \\\"verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T023507094543Z-goal_success-read-.iteris-goal_prompt.tx/log_manifest.json\\\",\\n  \\\"created_at\\\": \\\"2026-06-20T02:39:16.836120Z\\\",\\n  \\\"verifier\\\": \\\"iteris.codex_verification_agent\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T070357651402Z-assembly-stage-3-terminal-report-results/codex.events.jsonl:56:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' .iteris/goal_prompt.txt\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"/goal Stage-3 goal for the 2D Maxwell--PML energy-functional project.\\n\\nContext:\\nThe previous report results/energy_design_v2_report.md proposed a modified\\ncovariantly damped Maxwell--Debye absorbing-layer model and verified\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal E_\\\\rho(t)\\\\le \\\\mathcal E_\\\\rho(0),\\\\qquad t\\\\ge0.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is useful, but it is not enough for the next research step.\\n\\nThe next goal is not merely to re-state that the energy is bounded or\\nnon-increasing.  The next goal is:\\n\\n1. Verify whether the proposed modified PML model is actually perfectly\\n   matched.\\n2. If it is perfectly matched, give a complete formula-level proof.\\n3. If it is not perfectly matched, identify the exact mismatch and propose a\\n   repaired PML model that is closer to a standard stretched-coordinate,\\n   Berenger, CFS-PML, MPML, or Baffet-type construction.\\n4. For the accepted or repaired model, construct a genuinely monotone\\n   decreasing/dissipative energy functional and show the full derivation of its\\n   energy identity.\\n5. If no such monotone energy can exist for the model under consideration,\\n   provide a formula-level obstruction, not just a qualitative statement.\\n\\nImportant distinction:\\n- \\\"Non-increasing\\\" means dE/dt <= 0.\\n- \\\"Strictly decreasing\\\" or \\\"genuinely dissipative\\\" should mean\\n  dE/dt = -D(t) with D(t) >= 0 and with a clear explanation of when D(t)=0.\\n- If strict decay of the total energy on the whole computational domain is\\n  impossible because waves may remain in the undamped physical region, state\\n  this precisely and prove the obstruction with a concrete class of solutions.\\n- In that case, the acceptable target is a sharp dissipative identity plus a\\n  no-abnormal-growth/long-time-boundedness result, and the report must explain\\n  why this is the strongest possible statement.\\n\\nRequired sources to read before drawing conclusions:\\n1. sources/energy_functional_problem.tex\\n2. results/energy_functional_report_verified.md\\n3. results/energy_design_v2_report.md\\n4. results/energy_design_v2_report_verified.md\\n5. references/references.bib\\n6. The local PDFs in references/, especially:\\n   - references/Joly - 2012 - An elementary introduction to the construction and the analysis of perfectly matched layers for time.pdf\\n   - references/Baffet ... Energy Decay and Stability of a Perfectly Matched Layer For the Wave Equation.pdf\\n   - references/Be*cache ... Convergence analysis of time-domain PMLS for 2D electromagnetic wave propagation in dispersive waveg.pdf\\n   - references/Be*cache ... On a surprising instability result of Perfectly Matched Layers for Maxwell's equations in 3D media w.pdf\\n   - references/Huang ... explicit unconditionally stable finite element scheme for an equivalent Berenger's PML model.pdf\\n   - references/A_Nonconvolutional_Split-Field_Perfectly_Matched_L.pdf\\n   - references/Correia*Jin ... regular PML, CFS-PML, and second-order PML.pdf\\n   - references/Feng ... higher-order perfectly matched layer based on ADE method.pdf\\n   - references/Ping ... multiaxial perfectly matched layer M-PML.pdf\\n   - references/main.pdf\\n   - references/Super.pdf\\n   - references/Thesis.pdf\\n\\nDo not claim that a reference supports a conclusion unless you actually inspect\\nthe relevant model, theorem, proof idea, or formula.  The final report must\\ninclude a \\\"Literature reading record\\\" table listing each cited reference, the\\nsections/pages/formulas inspected, and exactly how it was used.\\n\\nMain technical tasks:\\n\\nA. Define a precise perfect-matching test.\\nStart with a planar interface and constant PML coefficients:\\n- physical half-space: rho_x=rho_y=0 or sigma_x=sigma_y=0;\\n- PML half-space, for example x>0 with rho_x>0, rho_y=0;\\n- tangential Fourier mode exp(i k_y y);\\n- Laplace or time-harmonic ansatz in time.\\n\\nDerive the transformed algebraic system, dispersion relation, normal wave\\nnumber, and wave impedance/admittance on both sides of the interface.\\n\\nPerfect matching should mean that for every outgoing physical mode entering\\nthe layer, the interface conditions admit a transmitted PML mode with zero\\nreflected amplitude.  Equivalently, the PML model should be representable as a\\ncomplex/stretched-coordinate continuation of the physical Maxwell--Debye\\nsystem without changing the interface impedance.\\n\\nB. Test the covariantly damped Maxwell--Debye model from v2.\\nUse the model\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty D_\\\\rho E\\n=\\\\partial_xH_y-\\\\partial_yH_x-D_\\\\rho P,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mu(\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho_y)H_x=-\\\\partial_yE,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mu(\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho_x)H_y=\\\\partial_xE,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nP+\\\\tau D_\\\\rho P=\\\\chi E,\\\\qquad D_\\\\rho=\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho,\\\\quad\\n\\\\rho=\\\\rho_x+\\\\rho_y.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nCheck whether this model is exactly matched in the sense of Task A.\\nIn particular, determine whether replacing the Debye law\\n\\\\[\\nP+\\\\tau\\\\partial_t P=\\\\chi E\\n\\\\]\\nby\\n\\\\[\\nP+\\\\tau(\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho)P=\\\\chi E\\n\\\\]\\npreserves the correct transformed material law or changes the interface\\nimpedance.  If it changes the impedance, compute the mismatch explicitly.\\n\\nC. If the v2 covariantly damped model is not perfectly matched, construct or\\npropose a repaired model.\\nAllowed approaches include:\\n1. Standard stretched-coordinate PML for Maxwell--Debye:\\n   derive it in the Laplace domain first, then introduce auxiliary variables\\n   for the time-domain model.\\n2. Berenger split-field PML or an equivalent unsplit auxiliary-variable form.\\n3. CFS-PML/ADE-PML if frequency shifting is needed for long-time behavior.\\n4. MPML-type damping if standard PML is unstable or not dissipative.\\n5. Baffet-type time-integrated variables or augmented variables to obtain a\\n   positive energy.\\n\\nThe repaired model must state:\\n- exact PDE system;\\n- boundary/interface conditions;\\n- assumptions on sigma_x, sigma_y or rho_x, rho_y;\\n- whether coefficients are constant, piecewise constant, or spatially varying;\\n- whether the model is perfect matching, stable only, or merely absorbing.\\n\\nD. Construct a monotone/dissipative energy functional.\\nFor each serious candidate model, give:\\n1. the exact candidate energy functional;\\n2. positivity proof, including conditions on coefficients and cross terms;\\n3. step-by-step multiplication/integration-by-parts calculation;\\n4. all boundary terms and why they vanish or have a sign;\\n5. final identity\\n   \\\\[\\n   \\\\frac{d}{dt}\\\\mathcal E(t)=-\\\\mathcal D(t)\\n   \\\\]\\n   or final inequality with every remainder term displayed;\\n6. classification:\\n   - strictly decreasing;\\n   - non-increasing with characterized nullspace;\\n   - dissipative but only after adding time-integrated variables;\\n   - uniformly bounded/no abnormal growth;\\n   - Gronwall only;\\n   - impossible, with formula-level obstruction.\\n\\nE. Address the relation to the old eight-variable model.\\nUse the verified old-model obstruction:\\n\\\\[\\nM_x=Q_x+\\\\mu\\\\frac{\\\\sigma_x}{\\\\epsilon_0}H_x=\\\\text{constant}\\n\\\\]\\non the x-edge zero-transverse mode and the resulting linear growth.  Explain\\nwhether the repaired model removes this mode, restricts it by compatibility\\nconditions, or changes the PML equations.\\n\\nF. Discuss Cole--Cole/fractional extension only after the alpha=1 perfect\\nmatching and energy questions are settled.\\nFor the fractional case, do not claim a theorem unless a positive memory\\nrepresentation is explicitly written.  State whether the fractional law helps\\nor cannot help with any electromagnetic/PML obstruction independent of P.\\n\\nRequired final artifact:\\nWrite the final report to:\\nresults/energy_design_v3_perfect_matching_report.md\\n\\nThe report should be written in Chinese, with formulas in LaTeX.\\n\\nRequired report structure:\\n\\n1. Executive summary\\n   - Is the v2 covariantly damped model perfectly matched?\\n   - Is a monotone/dissipative energy obtained?\\n   - What is the strongest verified conclusion?\\n\\n2. Literature reading record\\n   - Table: reference, inspected pages/sections/formulas, relevant result,\\n     how it affects the present problem.\\n\\n3. Perfect matching criterion\\n   - Planar interface setup;\\n   - Laplace/Fourier ansatz;\\n   - dispersion relation;\\n   - impedance/reflection coefficient criterion.\\n\\n4. Perfect matching test for the v2 model\\n   - full algebra;\\n   - conclusion;\\n   - if not matched, exact mismatch.\\n\\n5. Candidate repaired PML models\\n   - at least two candidates if the v2 model fails;\\n   - exact equations and matching/stability status.\\n\\n6. Energy functional construction\\n   - candidate energy;\\n   - positivity;\\n   - detailed derivative calculation;\\n   - final identity or inequality.\\n\\n7. Obstructions and impossibility statements\\n   - include explicit modes or algebraic blocks if monotone strict decay is\\n     impossible.\\n\\n8. Recommendation for the next manuscript step\\n   - which model to pursue;\\n   - what theorem statement looks feasible;\\n   - what remains open.\\n\\nVerification requirements:\\n- Use facts already verified by Iteris where applicable, but do not let them\\n  replace the new perfect-matching derivation.\\n- Verify any new algebraic claim needed for the final conclusion.\\n- If a reference PDF cannot be parsed or read, say so explicitly and mark the\\n  corresponding claim as unverified.\\n- Do not fabricate references, DOI numbers, theorem numbers, or page numbers.\\n\\nIteris goal contract:\\nProblem id: `2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration`.\\nTerminal artifact path: `results/energy_design_v3_perfect_matching_report.md`.\\n- Treat checkpoints as archival/recovery only; a checkpoint is not goal completion.\\n- Keep the project recoverable: after a substantial research artifact, promoted fact, verification result, or route change, run `iteris tool git checkpoint . --message \\\"checkpoint: <summary>\\\"`. Avoid checkpointing every small search.\\n- Keep working until EITHER (a) the terminal artifact exists, answers the requested goal, and real verification-agent assembly + goal-success verifications pass, OR (b) a principled stop is certified (see the PRINCIPLED STOP step below), unless the user explicitly stops the run.\\n- When you edit STATUS.md, bump its `last_updated:` header to the real edit time (UTC ISO-8601). A reader trusting a stale header is misled about how current the status is. For a long inline task you are working on yourself (no assigned sub-agent run), post a brief heartbeat into its `tasks/TASK_POOL.json` `notes` periodically so stale-task attention has positive liveness evidence rather than firing a false 'stalled' signal.\\n- Work persistently and bravely inside the same `/goal`: when one route is blocked, use the verified blocker to choose the next route rather than ending the run.\\n- Do not treat a verified blocker, gap report, or partial negative result as goal completion. Verified blockers are intermediate facts: record and verify them, then keep exploring alternate routes or repairing assumptions until the requested answer artifact itself passes assembly and goal-success verification. Narrowing the goal to a self-chosen sub-model or scope does NOT produce goal completion: success is judged against the SOURCE problem's full quantifier structure, so a narrowed result is an honest PARTIAL — finalize it via a principled stop (below), do not relabel it as a full solution.\\n- The terminal artifact should be concise and verifiable: include the original goal/problem summary, a fact index with `fact:` ids, each fact status/verification link, and a short assembly explaining how the facts answer the goal.\\n- Durable fact definition: a `fact:` is a stable, reusable claim that is absolutely true within its explicit assumptions and evidence scope. Do not use facts for mutable project state, route status, plans, priorities, literature impressions, or claims like what has been tried so far; put those in scratch memory, TASK_POOL, FRONTIER_INDEX, or artifacts.\\n- When a fact's statement or proof relies on earlier facts, record them with `--predecessor <fact:id>` (repeatable) at add-fact time. The dependency graph is what identifies keystone facts that deserve panel verification; a fact whose body cites `fact:` ids that are missing from its predecessors will fail validation cleanliness.\\n- A numerical experiment fact must declare its discriminating power: name the baseline/control (what a trivial, random, or fixed-instance baseline scores on the same instances) and what outcome would falsify the claim. An experiment that any baseline also passes is 0-discriminating and must not be cited as empirical support; design adversarial-vs-baseline instance suites, not pass-only suites.\\n- Certify before building on sampling evidence: finite instance sweeps, modular computations, and fitted curves are provisional, however many points agree. Before spawning route-building tasks anchored on such evidence, attempt a cheap exact certification (interval arithmetic, Sturm/Groebner certificates, exact rational evaluation at the relevant points) and record the certified scope in the fact; evidence that resists certification is a lead to test, not a foundation to build on.\\n- Use only verified facts in the final assembly. If a required fact fails verification, repair it, replace it, or mark it rejected and continue.\\n- Run real fact verification with `iteris tool verify submit . --backend agent --mode fact ... --json` for durable claims before relying on them in the final assembly. After a fact verification passes, use `iteris tool memory promote-fact . --fact-id <fact:id> --verification <request-id> --json`; do not hand-edit fact metadata unless the CLI is unavailable.\\n- `iteris tool verify submit . --backend agent ... --json` RUNS the verifier synchronously and RETURNS the verdict as JSON when it finishes (usually several minutes); read that returned JSON directly. NEVER background a verify submit with `&`, and NEVER write your own `sleep`/`until`/`while [ -f verification/results/... ]` shell loop to wait for a verdict: a hand-written waiter that misses the result file (or whose verifier died) deadlocks the entire `/goal` loop and the run stops making progress. Just let the submit command return.\\n- If you must wait on a verification that was submitted out-of-band (not by the submit you are currently running), use `iteris tool verify wait . --request-id <id> --timeout <seconds> --json`. It always returns: `status: done` with the verdict, `status: dead` (verifier gone — salvage with `iteris tool verify finalize . --request-id <id> --json` or resubmit), or `status: timeout`. Do not hand-roll this wait.\\n- Never park the loop on a single verification: verification agents can die silently, leaving a request with no result forever. If a request has no result after ~20 minutes, check `attention.stale_verifications` in `iteris tool context . --json`; when it reports the verifier dead, salvage with `iteris tool verify finalize . --request-id <id> --json` or resubmit, and keep doing other frontier work in the meantime.\\n- Use `tasks/TASK_POOL.json` as the work queue. Inspect it with `iteris tool task pool show . --json`, select work with `iteris tool task pool select-ready . --json`, and update status with `iteris tool task pool update . --task-id <task-id> --status ready|running|review|blocked|done|rejected|paused --json`. Use `done` for completed task-pool work.\\n- Act on the `attention` block in `iteris tool context . --json`. Stale running/review tasks are unharvested debt: inspect, harvest, or reset them before launching new work. A `rejection_streaks` entry means the same claim keeps failing verification; do NOT submit another proof revision of it. First run a falsification task against the claim itself (an execute agent whose objective is to find a counterexample or disprove it, especially near degenerate parameter regions), or change the decomposition. Resume direct proof attempts only after falsification fails. When more than 5 tasks sit in `review`, drain the review queue first — harvest each completed agent's output into facts/artifacts and mark the task done or rejected — before launching new execute agents; an unharvested output is work the project cannot use.\\n- Verification depth must scale with how load-bearing a fact is. An `attention.under_verified_keystones` entry marks a fact many later facts depend on (high in-degree: predecessors plus `fact:` citations in fact bodies) that has only one passed verification — a single flaw there collapses everything downstream. Before building further on a keystone fact, run `iteris tool verify panel . --mode fact --claim \\\"<fact claim>\\\" --artifact <fact-file> --fact-id <fact:id> --runs 2 --json` (independent adversarial verifiers; the panel passes only if every run passes) and repair or demote the fact if the panel fails.\\n- Operator messages are part of the loop: at every checkpoint and before starting a new task, check `unread_messages` in `iteris tool context . --json` (or run `iteris tool message list . --unread --json`). Act on high-priority messages before opening new work, and acknowledge every processed message with `iteris tool message ack . --msg-id <id> --disposition applied|noted|declined`; consuming a message's content without acking leaves the operator blind to whether it landed.\\n- To wait for a detached subagent, use `iteris tool agent wait . --run-id <run-id> --timeout <seconds> --json` instead of hand-written shell polling loops; it returns the terminal status and resolves dead workers instead of hanging.\\n- After every `iteris tool agent wait`/`iteris tool verify wait` returns — whether the work finished OR the wait timed out — re-check the inbox (`unread_messages` in `iteris tool context . --json`, or `iteris tool message list . --unread --json`) before launching the next step. A long sub-agent can park the loop inside one turn for many minutes; polling on each wait boundary keeps high-priority operator steers seen within one wait window instead of sitting unacked until the turn ends. Keep `--timeout` bounded (minutes, not hours) so the cadence stays tight.\\n- Keep `memory/facts/FRONTIER_INDEX.json` as the route map over facts. After major verified facts, rejected claims, or task-pool changes, run `iteris tool frontier refresh . --json`, then `iteris tool frontier health . --json`.\\n- If frontier health reports `explore_recommended: true`, launch `iteris tool agent explore . --focus \\\"<recommended_focus>\\\" --detach --json` before adding more execute tasks inside the same blocker pattern.\\n- Use subagents as background tools when useful: `iteris tool agent explore . --focus \\\"...\\\" --detach --json` for broad route discovery, `iteris tool agent execute . --task-id <task-id> --mode foundation|proof|experiment|algorithm --detach --json` for focused work, then inspect progress with `iteris tool agent runs . --json` and `iteris tool agent inspect . --run-id <run-id> --json`. Subagent completion is not goal completion; use their logs and outputs to update the task pool and frontier map.\\n- Use the structured artifact layout. Raw subagent logs stay in `artifacts/agent_runs/`. Route summaries belong in `artifacts/route_checks/`; proof attempts and verification claims in `artifacts/proofs/`; reproducible scripts/configs/raw outputs in `artifacts/experiments/`; reusable prototypes or implementation notes in `artifacts/code/`. Every subagent request includes an `artifact_workspace` and `artifact_manifest`; prefer those paths for new durable artifacts and list them in TASK_POOL expected outputs when adding tasks. Use `artifacts/ARTIFACT_INDEX.jsonl` as the global append-only artifact index and keep files grouped under `artifacts/<k