-covariantly-damped-maxwell-d/codex.events.jsonl:25:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The predecessor fact is verified and defines exactly the four-variable covariantly damped model used in the stage-3 calculation. I’m now checking the underlying source equations and sign conventions, because an admittance mismatch can flip if the TE Maxwell signs or Laplace convention differ.\"}}\n./verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T064341474739Z-fact-the-v2-covariantly-damped-maxwell-d/codex.events.jsonl:27:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' sources/energy_functional_problem.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\\\documentclass[UTF8,a4paper,12pt]{ctexart}\\n\\\\input{macros}\\n\\n\\\\title{Problem Sheet for Iteris: Energy Functionals for 2D Maxwell--PML}\\n\\\\author{Prepared source problem}\\n\\\\date{\\\\today}\\n\\n\\\\begin{document}\\n\\\\maketitle\\n\\n\\\\begin{abstract}\\nThis note is a self-contained source problem for an Iteris run.  The system to be analyzed is the two-dimensional TM Maxwell--PML model with polarization \\\\(P\\\\), PML auxiliary variables \\\\(P_{z,1},P_{z,2},Q_x,Q_y\\\\), and perfectly conducting boundary condition.  The first target is to find a genuinely monotone decreasing energy functional in two space dimensions.  If such a monotone energy cannot be found, the fallback target is to find a positive energy functional that proves boundedness/no abnormal long-time growth, in the same spirit as the long-time-stability role of MPML.  The non-fractional case should be understood as the special case \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\) of the displayed polarization law.\\n\\\\end{abstract}\\n\\n\\\\section{Primary Goal}\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\Om\\\\subset\\\\R^2\\\\) be a rectangular computational domain containing a physical region and a PML layer.  The absorption parameters are\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\sx=\\\\sx(x)\\\\ge0,\\\\qquad \\\\sy=\\\\sy(y)\\\\ge0,\\n\\\\]\\nor, in a harder variant,\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\sx=\\\\sx(x,y)\\\\ge0,\\\\qquad \\\\sy=\\\\sy(x,y)\\\\ge0.\\n\\\\]\\nThe main goal is to analyze the two-dimensional system in Section~\\\\ref{sec:model} and construct an energy functional \\\\(\\\\Ecal(t)\\\\).\\n\\n\\\\begin{enumerate}\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Strong target: monotone decreasing energy.}\\nFind a positive energy functional such that\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\frac{\\\\dd}{\\\\dd t}\\\\Ecal(t)=-\\\\Dcal(t)\\\\le0.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the preferred outcome.\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Fallback target: bounded/non-growing energy.}\\nIf no monotone energy can be found, find a positive energy functional that rules out abnormal growth from the PML layer, for example\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\Ecal(t)\\\\le C\\\\,\\\\Ecal(0)\\n\\\\]\\nwith \\\\(C\\\\) independent of long time if possible.  If only a Gronwall estimate\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\frac{\\\\dd}{\\\\dd t}\\\\Ecal(t)\\\\le C_\\\\sigma\\\\Ecal(t)\\n\\\\]\\nis available, identify the precise terms responsible for the possible growth and whether they are coefficient-gradient terms, corner-PML terms, or algebraic sign-indefinite terms.\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Useful obstruction.}\\nIf the strong target fails for natural quadratic energies, prove or at least sharply identify the obstruction.  An obstruction result is valuable if it explains which part of the PML coupling prevents monotonicity.\\n\\\\end{enumerate}\\n\\nThe energy may include weighted \\\\(L^2\\\\) norms, cross terms, shifted variables, time-integrated auxiliary variables, polarization energy, fractional-memory energy, and derivative terms if they are mathematically justified.\\n\\n\\\\section{Model to Analyze}\\n\\\\label{sec:model}\\n\\nThe model is the system shown in the supplied manuscript image.  Write\\n\\\\[\\n  E=E_z,\\\\qquad \\\\epsilon_\\\\infty=\\\\epsilon_{r,\\\\infty},\\\\qquad\\n  \\\\epsilon_s=\\\\epsilon_{r,s},\\\\qquad \\\\mu=\\\\mu_0\\\\mu_r,\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\chi=\\\\epsilon_0(\\\\epsilon_s-\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\nThe unknowns are\\n\\\\[\\n  E,\\\\quad H_x,\\\\quad H_y,\\\\quad P,\\\\quad P_{z,1},\\\\quad P_{z,2},\\\\quad Q_x,\\\\quad Q_y.\\n\\\\]\\nThe PML system is\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty\\\\,\\\\partial_t E\\n&= \\\\partial_xH_y-\\\\partial_yH_x\\n   -\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty(\\\\sx+\\\\sy)E\\n   -\\\\partial_tP-\\\\frac{\\\\sx+\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}P\\n   +P_{z,1}+P_{z,2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\mu\\\\,\\\\partial_tH_x\\n&= -\\\\partial_yE-\\\\mu\\\\frac{\\\\sy-\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0}H_x+Q_x,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\mu\\\\,\\\\partial_tH_y\\n&= \\\\partial_xE-\\\\mu\\\\frac{\\\\sx-\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}H_y+Q_y,\\\\\\\\\\nP+\\\\tau^\\\\alpha\\\\partial_t^\\\\alpha P\\n&=\\\\chi E,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tP_{z,1}\\n&=-\\\\frac{\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty}{\\\\epsilon_0}\\\\sx\\\\sy E,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tP_{z,2}\\n&=-\\\\frac{\\\\sx\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0^2}P,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tQ_x\\n&=-\\\\frac{\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0}Q_x\\n  +\\\\frac{\\\\mu\\\\sx(\\\\sy-\\\\sx)}{\\\\epsilon_0^2}H_x,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tQ_y\\n&=-\\\\frac{\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}Q_y\\n  +\\\\frac{\\\\mu\\\\sy(\\\\sx-\\\\sy)}{\\\\epsilon_0^2}H_y.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\label{eq:main-model}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nHere \\\\(\\\\partial_t^\\\\alpha\\\\) should be interpreted as a Caputo-type fractional derivative when \\\\(0<\\\\alpha<1\\\\).  The non-fractional case requested by the user is the case\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\alpha=1,\\\\qquad\\n  P+\\\\tau\\\\partial_tP=\\\\chi E.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the first stage of the analysis should use \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\), not a model with \\\\(P\\\\) removed.\\n\\nThe boundary condition is the perfectly conducting boundary condition\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\widehat n\\\\times E=0\\\\qquad\\\\text{on }\\\\partial\\\\Om.\\n\\\\]\\nFor the two-dimensional TM model this should be interpreted as\\n\\\\[\\n  E=E_z=0\\\\qquad\\\\text{on }\\\\partial\\\\Om,\\n\\\\]\\nso that the Maxwell principal part has no positive boundary contribution.  If a proof uses a different boundary condition, it must state that choice explicitly.\\n\\n\\\\begin{remark}\\nThe parameters \\\\(\\\\epsilon_0,\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty,\\\\epsilon_s,\\\\mu,\\\\tau\\\\) are positive, and \\\\(\\\\chi>0\\\\) when \\\\(\\\\epsilon_s>\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty\\\\).  The absorption parameters are time-independent and nonnegative.  The first serious cases to analyze are constant \\\\(\\\\sx,\\\\sy\\\\), then separated variable coefficients \\\\(\\\\sx(x),\\\\sy(y)\\\\), and only then general \\\\(\\\\sx(x,y),\\\\sy(x,y)\\\\).\\n\\\\end{remark}\\n\\n\\\\section{First Energy Balance to Verify}\\n\\nStart with the electromagnetic part\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\Ecal_{\\\\rm em}(t)=\\\\frac12\\\\int_\\\\Om\\n  \\\\left(\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty E^2+\\\\mu H_x^2+\\\\mu H_y^2\\\\right)\\\\dd x\\\\dd y.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Check the electromagnetic energy derivative]\\nUsing \\\\eqref{eq:main-model} and the PEC boundary condition, verify that the Maxwell principal part cancels by integration by parts, and that\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\frac{\\\\dd}{\\\\dd t}\\\\Ecal_{\\\\rm em}\\n=\\\\int_\\\\Om\\\\Big[\\n&-\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty(\\\\sx+\\\\sy)E^2\\n-E\\\\partial_tP\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\sx+\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}EP\\n+E(P_{z,1}+P_{z,2})\\\\\\\\\\n&-\\\\frac{\\\\mu}{\\\\epsilon_0}(\\\\sy-\\\\sx)H_x^2\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\mu}{\\\\epsilon_0}(\\\\sx-\\\\sy)H_y^2\\n+H_xQ_x+H_yQ_y\\n\\\\Big]\\\\dd x\\\\dd y .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\label{eq:em-balance}\\n\\\\]\\nThe terms involving \\\\(H_x,H_y\\\\) are sign-indefinite when \\\\(\\\\sx\\\\ne\\\\sy\\\\).  The terms involving \\\\(P,P_{z,1},P_{z,2}\\\\) are the electric/PML auxiliary coupling terms that must be absorbed by a refined energy.\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\section{The \\\\texorpdfstring{\\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\)}{alpha=1} Polarization Identity}\\n\\nFor the non-fractional case \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n  P+\\\\tau\\\\partial_tP=\\\\chi E,\\n\\\\]\\nso\\n\\\\[\\n  E=\\\\frac{P+\\\\tau\\\\partial_tP}{\\\\chi}.\\n\\\\]\\nThis gives the useful identity\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  E\\\\,\\\\partial_tP\\n  =\\\\frac{1}{2\\\\chi}\\\\frac{\\\\dd}{\\\\dd t}\\\\|P\\\\|_{L^2(\\\\Om)}^2\\n   +\\\\frac{\\\\tau}{\\\\chi}\\\\|\\\\partial_tP\\\\|_{L^2(\\\\Om)}^2.\\n\\\\label{eq:alpha-one-dissipation}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Use the alpha=1 identity]\\nUse \\\\eqref{eq:alpha-one-dissipation} to decide whether the energy\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\Ecal_{\\\\rm em+P}\\n  =\\\\Ecal_{\\\\rm em}+\\\\frac{1}{2\\\\chi}\\\\|P\\\\|_{L^2(\\\\Om)}^2\\n\\\\]\\nis the right starting point.  Then identify the remaining troublesome electric auxiliary terms:\\n\\\\[\\n  -\\\\frac{\\\\sx+\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}EP,\\\\qquad\\n  E P_{z,1},\\\\qquad\\n  E P_{z,2},\\\\qquad\\n  \\\\partial_tP_{z,1},\\\\qquad\\n  \\\\partial_tP_{z,2}.\\n\\\\]\\nTry to absorb them using cross terms, shifted variables, or time-integrated variables.\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\section{Magnetic Auxiliary Variable Shift}\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\n  a_x=\\\\frac{\\\\sy-\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0},\\\\qquad\\n  a_y=\\\\frac{\\\\sx-\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0},\\\\qquad\\n  b_x=\\\\frac{\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0},\\\\qquad\\n  b_y=\\\\frac{\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe magnetic equations and \\\\(Q_x,Q_y\\\\) equations suggest the shifted variables\\n\\\\[\\n  M_x=Q_x-\\\\mu a_xH_x,\\\\qquad\\n  M_y=Q_y-\\\\mu a_yH_y.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Verify shifted magnetic identities]\\nStarting from \\\\eqref{eq:main-model}, verify\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\partial_tM_x\\n&= a_x\\\\,\\\\partial_yE-\\\\frac{\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0}M_x,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_tM_y\\n&= -a_y\\\\,\\\\partial_xE-\\\\frac{\\\\sx}{\\\\epsilon_0}M_y.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\label{eq:magnetic-shift}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nThen test whether an energy written in terms of \\\\(H_x,H_y,M_x,M_y\\\\), rather than \\\\(H_x,H_y,Q_x,Q_y\\\\), has a cleaner dissipation structure.\\n\\\\end{task}\\n\\n\\\\begin{remark}\\nIf \\\\(\\\\sx,\\\\sy\\\\) are spatially variable, \\\\eqref{eq:magnetic-shift} itself does not produce \\\\(\\\\partial_t\\\\sigma\\\\) terms, because the coefficients are time-independent.  However, integrated energy identities may produce \\\\(\\\\partial_x\\\\sx\\\\), \\\\(\\\\partial_y\\\\sy\\\\), or general \\\\(\\\\nabla\\\\sigma_i\\\\) terms after integration by parts.  These terms are likely to be one source of bounded-but-not-monotone estimates.\\n\\\\end{remark}\\n\\n\\\\section{Electric PML Auxiliary Variables}\\n\\nThe variables \\\\(P_{z,1}\\\\) and \\\\(P_{z,2}\\\\) satisfy\\n\\\\[\\n  \\\\partial_tP_{z,1}=-\\\\frac{\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty}{\\\\epsilon_0}\\\\sx\\\\sy E,\\\\qquad\\n  \\\\partial_tP_{z,2}=-\\\\frac{\\\\sx\\\\sy}{\\\\epsilon_0^2}P.\\n\\\\]\\nThey appear in the \\\\(E\\\\)-equation as \\\\(E(P_{z,1}+P_{z,2})\\\\).  This resembles the need for time-integrated or cross-term energies in PML analyses.\\n\\n\\\\begin{task}[Handle Pz1 and Pz2]\\nTry the following routes:\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./verification/agent_runs/verify-20260620T064341474739Z-fact-the-v2-covariantly-damped-maxwell-d/codex.events.jsonl:29:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' artifacts/proofs/stage2_repair_design.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Stage 2 Repair Design for the Edge-PML Obstruction\\n\\nTask: `task-stage2-repair-design`\\n\\nPredecessor facts:\\n\\n- `fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:alpha1-algebra-balance`\\n- `fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:edge-pml-linear-obstruction`\\n- `fact:2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration:augmented-gronwall-energy`\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\nThis artifact starts from the verified alpha \\\\(=1\\\\) source model obstruction and\\ndoes not re-use that obstruction as final success.  It compares three repairs:\\n\\n1. a compatibility restriction on the old auxiliary data;\\n2. a shifted-variable MPML-style damping of the free magnetic auxiliary mode;\\n3. a covariantly damped Maxwell-Debye absorbing-layer model.\\n\\nThe third model is the strongest fully closed result below: it has a positive\\nmonotone energy identity for arbitrary data in its variables.  It is a modified\\nabsorbing model, not algebraically identical to the old eight-variable unsplit\\nPML formulation.\\n\\nThroughout this artifact\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\chi=\\\\epsilon_0(\\\\epsilon_s-\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty)>0,\\\\qquad\\nb_x=\\\\frac{\\\\sigma_x}{\\\\epsilon_0},\\\\quad b_y=\\\\frac{\\\\sigma_y}{\\\\epsilon_0},\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\na_x=\\\\frac{\\\\sigma_y-\\\\sigma_x}{\\\\epsilon_0}=b_y-b_x,\\\\qquad\\na_y=\\\\frac{\\\\sigma_x-\\\\sigma_y}{\\\\epsilon_0}=b_x-b_y.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n## Old-model obstruction term\\n\\nFor the source equations the shifted magnetic variables\\n\\\\[\\nM_x=Q_x-\\\\mu a_xH_x,\\\\qquad M_y=Q_y-\\\\mu a_yH_y\\n\\\\]\\nsatisfy\\n\\\\[\\nM_{x,t}=a_x\\\\partial_yE-b_yM_x,\\\\qquad\\nM_{y,t}=-a_y\\\\partial_xE-b_xM_y .\\n\\\\]\\nIn the \\\\(x\\\\)-edge case \\\\(\\\\sigma_x=s>0,\\\\sigma_y=0\\\\), the spatially constant\\nmode with\\n\\\\[\\nE=P=P_{z,1}=P_{z,2}=H_y=Q_y=0\\n\\\\]\\nhas \\\\(M_x=Q_x+\\\\mu b_xH_x\\\\) conserved.  Hence\\n\\\\[\\nH_x(t)=H_x(0)+\\\\frac{M_x(0)}{\\\\mu}t,\\\\qquad\\nQ_x(t)=Q_x(0)-b_xM_x(0)t .\\n\\\\]\\nThe obstruction is therefore the undamped shifted magnetic auxiliary \\\\(M_x\\\\) in\\nthe edge direction with zero transverse damping.  The \\\\(y\\\\)-edge case is\\nsymmetric with \\\\(M_y\\\\).\\n\\n## Repair 1: compatibility restriction on old auxiliaries\\n\\nThe weakest repair is to restrict the old model to a physical auxiliary\\nsubspace that excludes the verified free mode.  In the constant \\\\(x\\\\)-edge\\ncase this means\\n\\\\[\\nM_x(0)=Q_x(0)+\\\\mu b_xH_x(0)=0\\n\\\\]\\nfor the spatially constant edge mode; in the constant \\\\(y\\\\)-edge case it means\\n\\\\[\\nM_y(0)=Q_y(0)+\\\\mu b_yH_y(0)=0 .\\n\\\\]\\nThis compatibility condition is physically meaningful if the old \\\\(Q\\\\)\\nvariables are interpreted as derived PML histories rather than independent\\ninitial data.\\n\\nThe candidate energy for this restricted old model remains the positive\\naugmented source energy\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal E_0(t)\\n=\\\\frac12\\\\int_\\\\Omega\\\\left(\\n\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty E^2+\\\\mu H_x^2+\\\\mu H_y^2\\n+\\\\chi^{-1}P^2+P_{z,1}^2+P_{z,2}^2+Q_x^2+Q_y^2\\n\\\\right)\\\\,dxdy .\\n\\\\]\\nUnder only bounded nonnegative absorptions, the previous verified result gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal E_0'(t)\\\\le C_G\\\\mathcal E_0(t).\\n\\\\]\\nThe compatibility condition removes the exact linear edge mode in the\\none-dimensional constant-mode test.  It does not by itself give a closed\\ntwo-dimensional monotone energy, because in an \\\\(x\\\\)-edge\\n\\\\[\\nM_{x,t}=-b_x\\\\partial_yE\\n\\\\]\\nwhen \\\\(\\\\sigma_y=0\\\\).  Thus \\\\(M_x=0\\\\) is not invariant for general\\ntwo-dimensional fields unless an additional transverse condition\\n\\\\(\\\\partial_yE=0\\\\) is imposed.  Classification: useful compatibility repair for\\nthe old edge free mode, but not a complete 2D monotone repair.\\n\\n## Repair 2: shifted-variable MPML damping\\n\\nThe second repair keeps the shifted magnetic variables but adds a multi-axial\\nor CFS-style damping \\\\(\\\\eta_x,\\\\eta_y\\\\ge0\\\\) to the modes that were undamped at\\nedges.  The modified \\\\(Q\\\\) equations are\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{x,t}=-(b_x+\\\\eta_x)Q_x+\\\\mu(b_x+\\\\eta_x)a_xH_x,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{y,t}=-(b_y+\\\\eta_y)Q_y+\\\\mu(b_y+\\\\eta_y)a_yH_y,\\n\\\\]\\nwhile the \\\\(E,H,P,P_z\\\\) equations are unchanged.  Equivalently,\\n\\\\[\\nM_{x,t}=a_x\\\\partial_yE-(b_y+\\\\eta_x)M_x,\\n\\\\qquad\\nM_{y,t}=-a_y\\\\partial_xE-(b_x+\\\\eta_y)M_y .\\n\\\\]\\nThus the old \\\\(x\\\\)-edge constant-mode obstruction becomes\\n\\\\[\\nH_{x,t}=\\\\frac{M_x}{\\\\mu},\\\\qquad M_{x,t}=-\\\\eta_x M_x\\n\\\\]\\nwhen \\\\(E=0\\\\), \\\\(\\\\sigma_x>0\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\sigma_y=0\\\\).  If \\\\(\\\\eta_x>0\\\\), then for any\\nconstant \\\\(c_x>0\\\\)\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal E_{x,\\\\eta}\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\mu}{2}\\\\left|H_x+\\\\frac{M_x}{\\\\mu\\\\eta_x}\\\\right|^2\\n+\\\\frac{c_x}{2}|M_x|^2\\n\\\\]\\nis positive in \\\\((H_x,M_x)\\\\) and satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{d}{dt}\\\\mathcal E_{x,\\\\eta}=-c_x\\\\eta_x|M_x|^2\\\\le0 .\\n\\\\]\\nThe \\\\(y\\\\)-edge formula is identical with \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(y\\\\) interchanged.\\n\\nThis repair removes the specific linear-growth obstruction without imposing\\nthe compatibility \\\\(M_i(0)=0\\\\).  In the full two-dimensional system the energy\\nderivative still contains transverse derivative couplings\\n\\\\[\\nM_x\\\\partial_yE,\\\\qquad M_y\\\\partial_xE,\\n\\\\]\\nso a purely zeroth-order monotone energy is not closed by this shift alone.\\nWith derivative energies these terms are candidates for a dissipative\\nlower-order or Gronwall estimate.  Classification: monotone for the isolated\\nedge free subsystem, promising MPML repair for the old obstruction, but not a\\ncompleted full 2D monotone proof in this artifact.\\n\\n## Repair 3: covariantly damped Maxwell-Debye absorbing layer\\n\\nThe best closed repair is to replace the old independent \\\\(P_z,Q\\\\) auxiliary\\nsystem by a covariantly damped Maxwell-Debye absorbing layer.  Let\\n\\\\(\\\\rho_x,\\\\rho_y\\\\in L^\\\\infty(\\\\Omega)\\\\) be nonnegative, time-independent layer\\nprofiles that vanish in the physical region, and put\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\rho=\\\\rho_x+\\\\rho_y,\\\\qquad D_\\\\rho=\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho .\\n\\\\]\\nThe modified model is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty D_\\\\rho E\\n&=\\\\partial_xH_y-\\\\partial_yH_x-D_\\\\rho P,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\mu(\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho_y)H_x&=-\\\\partial_yE,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\mu(\\\\partial_t+\\\\rho_x)H_y&=\\\\partial_xE,\\\\\\\\\\nP+\\\\tau D_\\\\rho P&=\\\\chi E .\\n\\\\end{aligned}}\\n\\\\]\\nThe boundary condition is the same PEC condition,\\n\\\\[\\nE=0\\\\quad\\\\text{on }\\\\partial\\\\Omega .\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe model is local, agrees with the physical alpha \\\\(=1\\\\) Maxwell-Debye system\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\rho_x=\\\\rho_y=0\\\\), and damps the electric polarization by the same\\ncovariant derivative used in the layer equation.  This last point is the\\nessential repair of the old source-model term\\n\\\\(-((\\\\sigma_x+\\\\sigma_y)/\\\\epsilon_0)EP\\\\): the damping is applied to the\\nDebye law itself, so the polarization contribution becomes positive\\ndissipation rather than a sign-indefinite mixed product.\\n\\n### Candidate energy and positivity\\n\\nUse\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal E_\\\\rho(t)\\n=\\\\frac12\\\\int_\\\\Omega\\\\left(\\n\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty E^2+\\\\mu H_x^2+\\\\mu H_y^2+\\\\chi^{-1}P^2\\n\\\\right)\\\\,dxdy .\\n\\\\]\\nThis energy is positive definite in \\\\((E,H_x,H_y,P)\\\\) because\\n\\\\(\\\\epsilon_0,\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty,\\\\mu,\\\\chi>0\\\\).\\n\\n### Energy identity\\n\\nMultiplying the electric equation by \\\\(E\\\\), the magnetic equations by\\n\\\\(H_x,H_y\\\\), integrating over \\\\(\\\\Omega\\\\), and using \\\\(E=0\\\\) on\\n\\\\(\\\\partial\\\\Omega\\\\) gives cancellation of the Maxwell principal boundary flux:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int_\\\\Omega\\n\\\\left(E\\\\partial_xH_y-E\\\\partial_yH_x-H_x\\\\partial_yE+H_y\\\\partial_xE\\\\right)\\n\\\\,dxdy=0 .\\n\\\\]\\nThe covariant polarization law gives\\n\\\\[\\nE\\\\,D_\\\\rho P\\n=\\\\frac{1}{\\\\chi}P\\\\,D_\\\\rho P+\\\\frac{\\\\tau}{\\\\chi}|D_\\\\rho P|^2\\n=\\\\frac{1}{2\\\\chi}\\\\partial_t(P^2)\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\rho}{\\\\chi}P^2\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\tau}{\\\\chi}|D_\\\\rho P|^2 .\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\frac{d}{dt}\\\\mathcal E_\\\\rho(t)\\n=-\\\\int_\\\\Omega\\\\left(\\n\\\\epsilon_0\\\\epsilon_\\\\infty\\\\rho E^2\\n+\\\\mu\\\\rho_yH_x^2\\n+\\\\mu\\\\rho_xH_y^2\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\rho}{\\\\chi}P^2\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\tau}{\\\\chi}|D_\\\\rho P